  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1136 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nThanksgiving Day...Dry conditions are expected for Thanksgiving Day, \nbut the cloud shield from a low pressure system lifting northeast \nover the Ohio River Valley and Mid Atlantic region will result in \nmostly cloudy skies for the day. Anticipate the forecast area will \nremain dry Thursday night due to too much dry air in the low-levels. \nIf any sprinkles occur, they would be in the far southeast corner of \nthe forecast area (Manitowoc Co.), where there is a 15% chance or \nless of rain. Due to the close proximity of the low pressure system, \nthe pressure gradient will tighten over the Great Lakes, resulting \nin breezy conditions Thursday afternoon through Friday morning, \nmainly for eastern WI with gusts to 30 mph.\n\nFriday through Sunday...A ridge of high pressure will prevail over \nthe area on Friday, keeping conditions dry. A shortwave/trough and \nsurface boundary will quickly trail behind this ridge for Friday \nnight into Saturday morning. Thermal profiles suggest a wintry mix \nof snow/freezing rain/rain will be possible with the quick moving \nboundary. Given it's speedy movement, anticipate impacts would be \nminimal. However, cannot rule out a few slippery spots late Friday \nnight in central and north-central WI, since surface temps will be \nbelow freezing. By the second half of the weekend, the forecast area \nwill be split between a northern and southern stream system. Of the \ntwo, the southern stream system will be the stronger of the two, but \nit has been trending further south tracking across the Ohio River \nValley. This has caused PoPs to decrease for Sunday, with a 20% \nchance or less of rain in east-central WI. The northern stream \nsystem may generate some lake-effect snow showers across far north-\ncentral WI on Sunday, but the favorable northwest wind component \nappears to be lacking. If the snow occurs, minor travel impacts \nwould be possible given the high holiday traffic.\n\nRest of the extended...Dry conditions return for the start of the \nwork week as low-level ridging builds over the area. As the ridging \nexits into Tuesday, return southwest flow sets up with an area of \nlow-level f-gen developing over Upper MI. This looks to bring the \nnext chance for snow to portions of the Northwoods on Tuesday, with \nprobabilities for 0.5\" or more of snow of 15-20% in far north-\ncentral WI.\n\nTemperatures...High temperatures will be about 5-10 degrees above \nnormal for Thanksgiving Day through Saturday, with values mainly in \nthe 40s. Temperatures will take a dip near normal for Sunday and \nMonday with the passage of the late weekend systems, but will \nrebound back above normal for Tuesday and Wednesday.",
